$DIR/main.p:30:2: ticks = ... uses the per-CPU variable ticks outside a region where the task cannot move to another processor
    ticks has one copy for each processor. This is a store of a value that
    depends on the load of ticks at main.p:29, so the task could move to another
    processor in the middle of it and read one processor's copy and write
    another's. It must be inside a region where the task cannot move: preemption
    disabled (rt.DisablePreemption to rt.EnablePreemption), maskable interrupts
    disabled, or an rt.Lock held. A lone load or store of a whole variable of at
    most a machine word is allowed anywhere, as long as the store does not
    depend on a load of the same variable.
    Go has no per-processor variables. A Plutonium kernel keeps its processor's
    state in them, and an ordinary variable would be shared by every processor
    and need a lock. The compiler checks the uses instead, so that the most
    common use (a pointer to the running thread) stays safe code.
    Put the load and the store inside one region where the task cannot move:
        rt.DisablePreemption()
        defer rt.EnablePreemption()
        x := ticks
        ticks = x + 1
    (see "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2022" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:37:2: the access through a pointer to ticks uses the per-CPU variable ticks in a region that does not also hold the load it depends on
    ticks has one copy for each processor. This is an access through a pointer,
    which is not a lone store of a whole variable of at most a machine word, so
    the task could move to another processor in the middle of it and read one
    processor's copy and write another's. The store is inside a region, but the
    load it depends on was made outside that region (or in an earlier one), so
    the task could have moved between the two. The load and the store must be
    inside the same region. A lone load or store of a whole variable of at most
    a machine word is allowed anywhere, as long as the store does not depend on
    a load of the same variable.
    Go has no per-processor variables. A Plutonium kernel keeps its processor's
    state in them, and an ordinary variable would be shared by every processor
    and need a lock. The compiler checks the uses instead, so that the most
    common use (a pointer to the running thread) stays safe code.
    Put the load and the store inside one region where the task cannot move:
        rt.DisablePreemption()
        defer rt.EnablePreemption()
        x := ticks
        ticks = x + 1
    (see "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2022" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:46:2: pp.a = ... uses the per-CPU variable pp in a region that does not also hold the load it depends on
    pp has one copy for each processor. This is an access to part of the
    variable, so the task could move to another processor in the middle of it
    and read one processor's copy and write another's. The store is inside a
    region, but the load it depends on was made outside that region (or in an
    earlier one), so the task could have moved between the two. The load and the
    store must be inside the same region. A lone load or store of a whole
    variable of at most a machine word is allowed anywhere, as long as the store
    does not depend on a load of the same variable.
    Go has no per-processor variables. A Plutonium kernel keeps its processor's
    state in them, and an ordinary variable would be shared by every processor
    and need a lock. The compiler checks the uses instead, so that the most
    common use (a pointer to the running thread) stays safe code.
    Put the load and the store inside one region where the task cannot move:
        rt.DisablePreemption()
        defer rt.EnablePreemption()
        x := pp
        pp = x + 1
    (see "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2022" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:55:2: the call of g uses the per-CPU variable ticks outside a region where the task cannot move to another processor
    ticks has one copy for each processor. This is a call of g, which stores
    ticks, from a value or under a condition that depends on the load of ticks
    at main.p:54, so the task could move to another processor in the middle of
    it and read one processor's copy and write another's. It must be inside a
    region where the task cannot move: preemption disabled (rt.DisablePreemption
    to rt.EnablePreemption), maskable interrupts disabled, or an rt.Lock held.
    The dependence passes through a call of g, whose body the analysis cannot
    see; it assumes that the result of such a call depends on all of its
    arguments, and rejects the store when it cannot tell. A lone load or store
    of a whole variable of at most a machine word is allowed anywhere, as long
    as the store does not depend on a load of the same variable.
    Go has no per-processor variables. A Plutonium kernel keeps its processor's
    state in them, and an ordinary variable would be shared by every processor
    and need a lock. The compiler checks the uses instead, so that the most
    common use (a pointer to the running thread) stays safe code.
    Put the load and the store inside one region where the task cannot move:
        rt.DisablePreemption()
        defer rt.EnablePreemption()
        x := ticks
        g(x)
    (see "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2022" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:62:2: ticks = ... uses the per-CPU variable ticks outside a region where the task cannot move to another processor
    ticks has one copy for each processor. This is a store controlled by a
    condition that depends on the load of ticks at main.p:59, so the task could
    move to another processor in the middle of it and read one processor's copy
    and write another's. It must be inside a region where the task cannot move:
    preemption disabled (rt.DisablePreemption to rt.EnablePreemption), maskable
    interrupts disabled, or an rt.Lock held. A lone load or store of a whole
    variable of at most a machine word is allowed anywhere, as long as the store
    does not depend on a load of the same variable.
    Go has no per-processor variables. A Plutonium kernel keeps its processor's
    state in them, and an ordinary variable would be shared by every processor
    and need a lock. The compiler checks the uses instead, so that the most
    common use (a pointer to the running thread) stays safe code.
    Put the load and the store inside one region where the task cannot move:
        rt.DisablePreemption()
        defer rt.EnablePreemption()
        if ticks == old {
            ticks = new
        }
    (see "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2022" for a longer explanation)

